      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective study comparing the presence of antibodies against the psoriasis-associated antigen pso p27 in pain-free control subjects and patients with low back pain and/or sciatica."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To analyze the amount of local inflammation present in human lumbar disc disorders, using anti-pso p27 antibodies in the cerebrospinal fluid as a marker and to analyze whether pain intensity correlates with this marker of inflammation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Pso p27 is a major antigen in psoriasis that is also present, mostly locally, in other inflammatory disorders, such as sarcoidosis, inflammatory bowel disease, and ankylosing spondylitis, inflammation is also thought to play a major role in the generation of lumbar and radicular pain in degenerative disc disorders."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Anti-pso p27 antibodies in cerebrospinal fluid were quantified using an indirect enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay with pso p27 obtained from patients with psoriasis for use as an antigen. Fifteen patients with spinal stenosis, 11 patients without myelographic disc herniation, 17 patients with disc herniation, and 24 pain-free patient control subjects were studied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Significantly higher levels of anti-pso p27 antibodies were found in patients with myelographic signs of disc herniation than in with patients with no signs of herniation, patients with spinal stenosis, and control subjects. Patients with no known signs of disc herniation and patients with myelographic signs of spinal stenosis (< 10 mm in diameter) caused by degenerative changes, had higher levels of anti-pso p27 antibodies than did control subjects. However, these differences reached only borderline statistical significance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The results support those in previous reports, that inflammation probably plays an important role in degenerative disk disorders, particularly in disk herniations. That there was no correlation between pain intensity and anti-pso p27 activity indicates that the antigen is probably not essential in pain generation per se. The results may indicate that pso p27 is expressed secondary to, not as an initiator of, inflammation."
        }
      ]},
